# Trivy ignore file — HIGH/CRITICAL CVEs we consciously accept, with a reason.
# The deploy Trivy gate uses `ignore-unfixed: true`, so only FIXABLE HIGH/CRITICAL
# reach here. Keep this list tiny and justified; re-check on every pnpm bump.

# pnpm — Repository-controlled configDependencies can select a package's native
# install engine. Fixed ONLY in pnpm 11.5.3 (no 10.x backport per Trivy DB).
# We pin pnpm to 10.34.x, which clears every OTHER pnpm CVE from this advisory
# batch (CVE-2026-50015/55487/55698, GHSA-72r4/qrv3). We deliberately stay on
# pnpm 10.x because pnpm 11 (a) drops `pnpm.overrides` from package.json and
# (b) adds a `minimumReleaseAge` policy that rejects the AWS SDK — a larger,
# separate migration.
# Not exploitable in our pipeline: every install runs `--frozen-lockfile`
# against OUR committed lockfile (no attacker-supplied manifest), and we do not
# use `configDependencies`. Revisit when we migrate to pnpm 11.x.
CVE-2026-55697

# corepack's BUNDLED pnpm distribution (/opt/corepack/v1/pnpm/10.34.4/dist/
# node_modules/) vendors its own copies of these. They are NOT in our lockfile,
# so `pnpm.overrides` cannot reach them — the only real fixes are a pnpm release
# that re-bundles them, or dropping corepack from the runtime image (which the
# base stage's `RUN pnpm --version` guard deliberately prevents; see
# apps/api/Dockerfile).
#
# Not exploitable here: both are denial-of-service via attacker-supplied input
# to pnpm ITSELF, and pnpm never runs at runtime (the CMD is a direct `node`
# invocation, no pnpm/sh wrapper). At build time pnpm only installs from OUR
# committed lockfile with --frozen-lockfile, so no attacker-controlled tarball
# or manifest ever reaches its tar/glob code paths.
#
# OUR OWN copies of these packages ARE patched via pnpm.overrides in the root
# package.json (brace-expansion 1.1.16 / 2.1.2 / 5.0.8, js-yaml 4.3.0) — this
# entry covers only the vendored duplicates inside the pnpm bundle.
# Re-check on every pnpm bump; drop these lines once the bundle ships fixes.
CVE-2026-59873
CVE-2026-59874
CVE-2026-13149

# brace-expansion — DoS via a crafted brace pattern.
#
# CORRECTED 2026-08-06. This entry used to claim that 1.1.16 and 2.1.2 were the
# last releases of their lines with no backport, so nothing could be done. That
# expired: 1.1.18, 2.1.4 and 5.0.9 have since shipped, and pnpm.overrides now
# pins all three (`brace-expansion@1` ^1.1.18, `@2` ^2.1.4, `@5` ^5.0.9). Every
# copy in pnpm-lock.yaml is patched — including the archiver@5 / exceljs chain
# the old note called unfixable.
#
# What remains is ONE copy, and only the one: brace-expansion 2.1.1 vendored
# inside corepack's own pnpm bundle (/opt/corepack/v1/pnpm/10.34.4/dist/
# node_modules/), which is not in our lockfile and cannot be reached by
# pnpm.overrides — the same situation as the entries above it.
#
# Not exploitable here: brace-expansion is reached through minimatch/glob doing
# FILE PATH matching, and inside the pnpm bundle those patterns are pnpm's own
# internal globs over our committed lockfile. pnpm never runs at runtime (the
# CMD is a direct `node` invocation), so no request body or uploaded filename is
# ever used as a brace pattern.
#
# DROP BOTH LINES once corepack ships a pnpm bundle with a patched copy.
# Re-check on every pnpm bump.
CVE-2026-14257
CVE-2026-69152

# ip-address — inconsistent IP parsing leading to SSRF. Fixed in 10.3.1, and
# 10.2.0 is present ONLY inside corepack's pnpm bundle (pnpm vendors it via
# socks-proxy-agent for registry access). It is not in pnpm-lock.yaml — an
# override for it resolves to nothing, which is why one is not added here.
#
# Not exploitable here: the SSRF requires attacker control over the address
# being parsed. The only consumer is pnpm's own proxy stack talking to the
# registry we configure, at BUILD time; pnpm is never invoked at runtime, and no
# customer-supplied URL reaches it. The application's own outbound fetches
# (provider calls, outbound webhooks) go through the SSRF-safe fetch in
# lib/http, which does not use this package.
#
# DROP once corepack ships a pnpm bundle on ip-address >= 10.3.1.
CVE-2026-69192
